# 9. CONTENT INTEGRITY AND ISLAMIC EDUCATIONAL SAFETY

This is a non-negotiable requirement.

The application must not invent Quranic text, translations, hadith quotations, tafsir statements, scholarly endorsements, or religious rulings.

### Source policy

- Use a vetted Arabic Quran text from a reliable, properly licensed source.
- Use authorized translations appropriate to Indonesia and Malaysia.
- Identify the translation edition and source.
- Use approved recitation audio with appropriate licensing.
- Store verse references with the content.
- Keep a clear editorial approval status for religious educational content.
- Identify the source for any published tafsir or hadith excerpt.
- Review transliteration, diacritics, word boundaries, morphology, and language explanations.
- If a source is unavailable or unverified, show that the content needs review rather than fabricating it.

AI may assist with general language explanations and guided practice, but must not be presented as an independent religious authority.

Any AI-generated explanation touching Quranic interpretation must be constrained by approved sources, reviewed content, and transparent attribution.

Do not present a learner's personal reflections as tafsir.

Avoid claims that completing the course guarantees deep Quran comprehension or perfect recitation.

Never use guilt, religious fear, or fabricated spiritual rewards as engagement mechanisms.

### Sample content

Create a small, well-structured demonstration lesson using verified educational data.

Clearly mark any demonstration content that has not been editorially approved.

Do not fill hundreds of lessons with invented Quranic explanations simply to make the course library look populated.

Create reusable content structures and a curriculum framework that can be populated and reviewed by qualified educators.

